| Field                 | Type   | Required | Description                                                                                                    |
|-----------------------|--------|----------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| variable              | string | No       | Name of a global variable of type "execute target list" to reference                                           |
| host_list             | array  | No       | Static host list                                                                                               |
| dynamic_group_list    | array  | No       | Dynamic group list                                                                                             |
| topo_node_list        | array  | No       | CMDB topology node list                                                                                        |
| container_list        | array  | No       | Static container list                                                                                          |
| container_filter_list | array  | No       | Container filter list; multiple filters are unioned                                                            |

An execute target is either a variable reference or a set of concrete targets, and the two are **mutually
exclusive**: either provide `variable` alone, or provide at least one non-empty value among the other five fields.
Providing both returns a parameter error, and providing neither does as well.

> The default value of a global variable (`global_var_list[].execute_target`) supports concrete targets only. It
> does not accept `variable`, because a variable default must not reference another variable.

##### host_list[] element

{% include '_generic_v4_host.md.j2' %}

##### dynamic_group_list[] element

| Field | Type   | Required | Description      |
|-------|--------|----------|------------------|
| id    | string | Yes      | Dynamic group ID |

##### topo_node_list[] element

| Field     | Type   | Required | Description                                                            |
|-----------|--------|----------|------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| id        | long   | Yes      | Topology node ID                                                       |
| node_type | string | Yes      | Topology node type. Allowed values: `module` (module), `set` (set)     |

##### container_list[] element

| Field        | Type | Required | Description                        |
|--------------|------|----------|------------------------------------|
| container_id | long | Yes      | Container ID (CMDB internal ID)    |

Only `container_id` is taken on write. The read API additionally returns `container_uid`, `name`, `pod_name`,
`namespace`, `cluster_uid` and `node_ip`, which come from the snapshot taken at save time and are ignored on write,
so a read response can be written back as is.

Container IDs are not validated on write. If a container does not exist or does not belong to the current scope,
the read API still returns what was saved, but executing the job reports "invalid execute object" together with the
offending `container_id`.

##### container_filter_list[] element

| Field           | Type   | Required | Description                                                                       |
|-----------------|--------|----------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| name            | string | No       | Filter name, at most 60 characters, only used to tell multiple filters apart        |
| kube_topo_list  | array  | Yes      | Topology path list, must not be empty; paths are unioned and share one `prop_conditions` |
| prop_conditions | array  | No       | Container property conditions, intersected with each other                          |

> The topology here is expressed with CMDB internal numeric IDs, which differs from `kube_container_filters` in APIs
> such as execute job (cluster UID and namespace name). The two shapes are not interchangeable.

###### kube_topo_list[] element

| Field         | Type   | Required | Description                                                                                     |
|---------------|--------|----------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| cluster       | object | Yes      | Cluster topology object, e.g. `{"id": 105}`                                                     |
| namespace     | object | No       | Namespace topology object, e.g. `{"id": 207}`; omit it to not narrow down by namespace          |
| workload_list | array  | No       | Workload topology objects, e.g. `{"kind": "deployment", "id": 309}`; omit it to not narrow down by workload |

Cluster, namespace and workload IDs are all CMDB internal IDs, which can be obtained from the read API after
configuring the step once on the web console. They are not validated on write; if they do not exist or do not belong
to the current scope, executing the step reports "target execute object is empty" because no container matches.

###### prop_conditions[] element

| Field | Type   | Required | Description                                   |
|-------|--------|----------|-----------------------------------------------|
| field | string | Yes      | Field to filter on, see the table below       |
| value | string | Yes      | Value, matched as described in the table below |

**This API does not accept an operator.** The match type of each field is fixed and filled in by the server, exactly
as the web console does, so conditions created through this API render correctly on the web console:

| field                     | Match type      | Description                             |
|---------------------------|-----------------|-----------------------------------------|
| `container_name`          | contains        | Container name contains `value`         |
| `container_container_uid` | equals          | Container runtime UID equals `value`    |
| `pod_name`                | contains        | Pod name contains `value`               |
| `pod_labels`              | label selector  | Pod labels match the `value` expression |

For the first three fields, `value` accepts comma-separated multiple values, matched as a union. For example
`nginx,redis` matches names containing either `nginx` or `redis`.

**The `value` of `pod_labels` is a native Kubernetes label selector expression**, not a plain string. See
[Kubernetes documentation: Label selectors](https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/overview/working-with-objects/labels/#label-selectors)
for the syntax. Both equality-based and set-based selectors are supported. A comma there is the AND separator of the
expression itself and is **not** treated as a multi-value separator. The expression is parsed and validated by this
API; an invalid one returns 400. Examples:

- `app=nginx` — label `app` equals `nginx`
- `app=nginx,tier!=frontend` — both requirements must hold
- `env in (prod, pre)` — `env` is one of the given values
- `!canary` — the `canary` label must not exist
